			<div class="content" id="post-content-4819113">
									

<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">I&nbsp;hope&nbsp;I&nbsp;did&nbsp;this&nbsp;collapsible&nbsp;right</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">This&nbsp;should&nbsp;mean&nbsp;yes!</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>As far as containment, I have been mulling this over for a while now, and I think in the end the final containment procedures will result in my SCP being basically dormant. Part of my initial idea was that it only ever &quot;leaves&quot; if nobody is watching (though it can still be heard), and I think in the end having some form of visual surveillance should be enough to keep it where it is. (also considering having it break this rule and interfere with surveillance in a single instance, but never to do it again. Not 100% sure I'm gonna go with that yet but I currently intend to).</p>
<p>Ok so it's been a while since I've thought about all of this, because I had given up on the idea for a while, but here's what I think my ideas were. The man in the suit is actually the only thing living about the SCP. The car itself seems to be autonomous, but in reality it is just the part of the anomaly that is always visible. The guy, which would end up being SCP-XXXX-1 I suppose, is much harder to connect to the anomaly, but the foundation catches wind of him being somehow related in the end. Anything the car &quot;collects&quot; is just something the suited man has brought back with him. When the car visits a place, it actually shows up parked there, and can be viewed for the duration of it's stay. Sometimes the man is also spotted around this time, but part of the anomaly is that nobody can remember what he looks like aside from very basic things, such as his attire.</p>
<p>Now I don't mean any of this to say that the car isn't anomalous itself. Aside from it's connection to this entity, it also has the capability to be driven through and on any material. The only way this is shown, however, is through the tire marks &quot;leaving&quot; it's containment that are found either before the foundation fully understands how to keep it where it is, or during testing. It is kept in a simple concrete room underground, given that it just seems to be a self driving car at first, but the room isn't itself enough to keep it in place.</p>
<p>I personally like the idea just because I feel it has a bit of whimsy to it at first appearing to be a car with a personality, but with some cool depth beyond that.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>I hope I've explained my ideas well. It feels sloppy trying to explain it quickly like this, and there are probably things I need to solidify, but I'm hoping by talking about it here I'm doing it the right way. I'm glad to be met with positive feedback this time! I think last time I was just unlucky. Multiple butterfly squad members I contacted were busy, and the only reviewer that found it didn't seem to care much. It was just an unfortunate stroke of fate combined with another. I'm very glad after the genuine replies I've gotten so far, and I am hopeful that I can make this idea work!</p>

							</div>

								
					
