			<div class="content" id="post-content-4817840">
									

<p>Short version: You've got the bones of a simple but workable idea here, with the caveat that you'll need to steer around some cliches worn deep enough to break an axle.</p>
Long version:
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Calculating&nbsp;most&nbsp;direct&nbsp;route...</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Starting&nbsp;navigation</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>As mentioned, it's simple, but that's not inherently a bad thing.</p>
<p>The concept lends itself heavily toward the classic site trope where we get a story delivered via snapshots and fragmented details in a series of logs. Just to be upfront and honest, even a hypothetical perfect version would almost certainly get downvote comments about &quot;cliche log article&quot;. The formula has survived, however, by being a lot of fun when it's well executed- meaning you want the logs to be interesting individually, occasionally surprising, and ultimately come together to suggest a bigger picture that elevates it beyond the formula.(Preferably with a bit of excitement and a twist or two along the way)</p>
<p>I definitely wouldn't limit destinations to places on Earth- there's all sorts of amusing antics that can ensue if road trips occasionally take it through the late Cretaceous, the Plane of Cheese, or to the Moon.</p>
<p>I really like the souvenir aspect, despite (or maybe because of) an &quot;item generator&quot; being another heavily worn cliche. It makes sense with the theme, and opens up potential for some old-school weirdness. I'd suggest something like having all the items be &quot;tacky gift shop&quot;-type items related to the destination (an &quot;I went to Delaware&quot; T-shirt, a dusty refrigerator magnet of <a href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Earthrise" onclick="window.open(this.href, '_blank'); return false;">Earthrise</a>, a 4 dimensional paperweight&#8230;) and I wouldn't make them a central focus of the SCP so much as an interesting way hint at where the thing went on its stranger trips, and/or to add some deadpan humor here and there.</p>
<p>For narrative, I really think you could just skirt around how it was found, unless that tells us something relevant to the story you're weaving. That part about the mysterious guy in the brown suit is where my gut says to focus your development efforts- how he relates to this vehicle and it's properties, how he reacts to any passengers, etc. Best tip I can give there: figure out what the obvious direction to develop him would be, and then do almost the opposite of that.</p>
<p>The foundation is curious enough that they'll want to know where this thing is going and why, so it's a logical advancement that they'd eventually start strapping cameras to the thing and having MTFs and D-Class hitch rides. At some point people probably need to get out and explore for maximum weirdness to ensue, and also because the mental image of a squad desperately trying to get back into a moving model T to escape [redacted] while the car phases through a building before driving down a sheer cliff.</p>
<p>Overall, it's not quite to a greenlight yet, but I think there's something here that can be refined into a viable SCP.</p>
<p>One additional idea I'll throw out there that could maybe help bind this all together or spark more ideas: mechanical navigators like the The Jones Live Map and the Inter-Auto. These were devices, created at around the same time as the model T, which functioned much like modern turn-by-turn GPS systems. Depending on the device, you'd insert something like a scrolling paper map or a wheel of instructions and hit &quot;start&quot; mechanism at a given landmark- from there the device, using an odometer, would advance the instructions as you went. (One ambitious brand even used a cylinder phonograph to do audio announcements. No word on the quality, mind you.)</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>On your experience last time: If you want more critique on any idea, see <a href="http://www.scpwiki.com/forum/t-13282692/how-to-get-more-reviews">this post</a> for how to reach out to reviewers for input. Especially don't feel abandoned or discouraged by getting only a single review from a particular reviewer- even really active people get busy or need to take a break now and then, miss followup questions on the forums, or have other reasons for not following up.<sup class="footnoteref"><a id="footnoteref-390498-1" href="javascript:;" class="footnoteref" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnote-390498-1')">1</a></sup></p>
