															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/owltamer" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(5409628); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=5409628&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606056696" alt="Owltamer" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=5409628)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/owltamer" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(5409628); return false;" >Owltamer</a></span> <span class="odate time_1605442479 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">15 Nov 2020 12:14</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4819127">
									

<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Got&nbsp;the&nbsp;hang&nbsp;of&nbsp;this&nbsp;collapsible&nbsp;thing&nbsp;in&nbsp;my&nbsp;last&nbsp;post,&nbsp;so&nbsp;it&nbsp;should&nbsp;be&nbsp;fine&nbsp;here...</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Success!</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>Where to begin?</p>
<p>As far as structure, I think some of it can be told without logs, but you are right in that I think I will still be telling most of the narrative, or at least a good portion, with logs. As of right now I can't think of a workaround to this, but I'm hoping it doesn't need one.</p>
<p>It's funny that you mention &quot;tacky gift shop&quot; as this is one of the initial ideas I had for the first place where the suited man is spotted! Before this instance, I intended for the foundation to have not realized yet that a humanoid entity is even related to the vehicle whatsoever. But after being able to track down eyewitnesses at the location of the car's most recent visit, one store clerk gives an account of having sold a knicknack to a man in a suit and bowlers hat, which happens to be the same one found in the car after said trip.</p>
<p>As far as other things the car has brought back, they are generally aimed at similar quirky things, but all creating a kind of personality for the entity, first believed to be the car itself, but later revealed to be its mysterious driver.</p>
<p>I had actually intended for the car to be undriveable by individuals other than the suited man. Probably something simple, like it won't start. The anomalous property of it being able to traverse anything is only understood when:</p>
<p>A: Tire marks peeling out of it's resting spot lead directly into a wall, facing the direction of it's final target regardless of actual traverable route.<br />
B: It returns with an item very clearly from whatever place it had been facing when it left.</p>
<p>I am glad you mention how the foundation would react to such an SCP, because I have actually been wondering myself. I know the foundation likes to understand it's anomalies as best as possible, but I know their top objectives is to &quot;contain.&quot; I was worried that the narrative for these logs would have to be told through the idea of the car having &quot;broken out&quot; of its containment, but it would make things much easier if the foundation had actually been letting it happen in order to understand the phenomenon better. This is where my rookie knowledge of the foundation comes in though, and I could really use a better versed mind here. How would the foundation treat this SCP? Would they immediately try to stifle the anomalous properties given it must leave in order for them to arise. Would they be ok with it taking place if under conditions they believe best for testing? It's completely non-dangerous, and it always returns to where it was parked originally (the facility in this instance), but would they be ok with it leaving at all? I can work around this to make the only logs take place before they understand that it is leaving at all, but it would limit the possibilities given this restriction. If testing is completely on the table, things are a million times easier. I just want whatever is more in line with a believable story.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Thanks for all of your thoughts. It means a lot to have such a detailed look at my idea. I hope this SCP works out in the end. I have a strong connection to it now after having come up with it so long ago, and I have always wanted to contribute an SCP to the overarching lore, and hopefully this will be the first of many.</p>

							</div>

								
					
