				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/scorpion451" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1918720); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1918720&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606056696" alt="Scorpion451" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1918720)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/scorpion451" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1918720); return false;" >Scorpion451</a></span> <span class="odate time_1605483034 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">15 Nov 2020 23:30</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4819536">
									

<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Happy&nbsp;to&nbsp;oblige</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Back&nbsp;on&nbsp;the&nbsp;road:</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>As far as structure, I think some of it can be told without logs, but you are right in that I think I will still be telling most of the narrative, or at least a good portion, with logs. As of right now I can't think of a workaround to this, but I'm hoping it doesn't need one.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is definitely a viable direction to go- many SCPs take this sort of log-centric approach to their storytelling. I'll get more into this below, but in-universe the Foundation is <em>very</em> big on research and record-keeping, and out-of-universe things like exploration logs and documentation addenda classic ways to play with the format- <a href="/scp-093">SCP-093</a>, <a href="/scp-610">SCP-610</a>, and <a href="/scp-1730">SCP-1730</a> are just a few examples of what can be done when you really pull out the stops. (One of these days I'm going to find the time to adapt 1730 as a graphic novel, because it deserves one.)</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I had actually intended for the car to be undriveable by individuals other than the suited man. Probably something simple, like it won't start.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Picked up on that, it's totally reasonable. I was thinking more that the Foundation could have some passengers tag along, after trying cameras a few times. (Up to you whether they get anything useful from the cameras, but they'd at least <em>try</em> cameras first before sending in people.)</p>
<blockquote>
<p>How would the foundation treat this SCP?</p>
</blockquote>
<p><a href="/scp-2703">SCP-2703</a> would be a good example to look at as nice simple crash course in how the Foundation handles uncontainable entities and how they research the ones they're trying to understand.</p>
<p>For containment, they focus on keeping it from drawing too much attention from civilians, and experiment-wise, they're trying to keep it cooperative while they figure out what it is and what it wants. The procedures and description give us the technical details and our first hints of the strangeness, and then the logs give us a nice little sketch of it's personality and a miniature story arc with a cute happy-ish ending.</p>
<p>For your car, containment probably means people following it around making sure it doesn't cause traffic accidents and minimizing the number of people who see it drive through walls, and experimentation/exploration means figuring out where it's going and why. There's a bunch of stories you can tell here and as many ways to tell it, but the big thing is to focus on what 2703 does in just a few entries- a cautious, logical exploration of a strange thing in an interesting way.</p>
<p>I'll point at <a href="/scp-1306">SCP-1306</a>, <a href="/scp-1472">SCP-1472</a>, and <a href="/scp-3044">SCP-3044</a>, meanwhile, as good examples of the fun you can have with longer testing logs in the short form, and how they're a great way to maximize the rule &quot;show don't tell&quot; in a format that's all about &quot;tell&quot; on the surface. If you include short-form logs of that sort (say, the camera stage of exploration is presented via brief summaries of recorded footage and the retrieved souvenir for each trip), I'd suggest thinking of each entry as a puzzle piece that we can put together to figure out more about bowler hat guy and the car, then use any exploration with people to play the &quot;answer questions in ways that give us more questions&quot; game.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

